\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The scalar Gaussian integral}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{For real #{a}, consider the extended nonnegative Lebesgue integral
##{I(a)=\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-a x^2/2}\,dx.}
For #{a>0} it is finite and positive, and
##{I(a)=\sqrt{2\pi /a},\qquad I(1)^2=2\pi.}
For #{a\leq0} it is infinite.}
\proof{
\p{Suppose #{a>0}. The continuous integrand is at most one on
#{[-1,1]}. For #{|x|\geq1}, it is at most #{e^{-a|x|/2}}, whose
integral on #{\mathbb R} is #{4/a}. This proves finiteness before
evaluation. On #{[-1,1]} the integrand is at least #{e^{-a/2}}, giving
the strictly positive lower bound #{2e^{-a/2}}.}
\p{Set #{J=\int_0^\infty e^{-x^2}\,dx}, likewise finite and positive.
For each fixed #{y>0}, the substitution #{x=yt} gives
#{J=\int_0^\infty y e^{-y^2t^2}\,dt}. Multiply by #{e^{-y^2}},
integrate in #{y}, and apply nonnegative [Tonelli](fdip-002Q):
##{\begin{aligned}
J^2&=\int_0^\infty \int_0^\infty
           y e^{-(1+t^2)y^2}\,dy\,dt\\
   &=\int_0^\infty\frac{dt}{2(1+t^2)}=\frac\pi4.
\end{aligned}}
The inner primitive is #{-e^{-(1+t^2)y^2}/(2(1+t^2))}; the final
integral is evaluated by arctangent. Thus #{J=\sqrt{\pi}/2}.
Reflection and the positive substitution #{x=\sqrt{2/a}\,z} give
the asserted value of #{I(a)}. If #{a\leq0}, the integrand is at
least one everywhere and its nonnegative integral is infinite.}
}
\p{The calculation adapts Keith Conrad's improper-integral argument in
\citet{Section 2, Second proof: Another change of variables, PDF pp. 1-2}{conradGaussianIntegral}
to Lebesgue integration. The explicit tail bound and nonnegative Tonelli
justify the adaptation; Tonelli is
\citet{Theorem 1.7.15, pp. 200-201, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
Mathlib's \leanref{integrable_exp_neg_mul_sq} and
\leanref{integral_gaussian} support the real one-variable result with
coefficient #{b=a/2>0}. The value theorem uses a totalized integral;
its value at a nonintegrable parameter does not mean the nonnegative
normalizer is finite.}
